there have been no passenger flights at  east midlands airport since  early february the departure board is  blank  the check-in desks empty safe for  essential maintenance  paul trajar has worked here for 12 years  he's calibrating the luggage weighing  scales  hoping they'll soon be logging the bags  of returning travelers  we desperately desperately need  passengers back in the building you know  to keep keep the economy working and  keep you know keep the airport alive  the government is considering reducing  air passenger duty on internal flights  to make fares cheaper and improve  connectivity around the uk  you pay less tax if you're flying to say  london to barcelona than you do if  you're flying london to  edinburgh and which and back on the  return journey which is a  somewhat bizarre situation the aviation  industry welcomes anything to get planes  taking off  good news for uh regional connectivity  in the uk it's good for  uh the uk aviation sector that's had a  really torrid time  since uh kovid hit 12 months ago  but airports present the government with  a dilemma  this is what green campaigners want to  see  nobody is flying but the government has  to balance the urgent need to get the  economy back on its feet  with its long-term climate change goals  the uk has a legally binding target to  reach net zero emissions by 2050.  last year the government pledged to  reduce emissions by at least  68 percent by 2030 compared to 1990  levels  more than a third of the uk's emissions  come from transport  including domestic air travel the  government has faced recent criticism  for failing to intervene  over plans for a new coal mine along  with frustration  over a lack of policies to reduce  emissions in last week's budget  the climate stuff just seems to go out  of the window  in favor of the short-term economic or  political interests  and we really haven't got time to keep  doing that we've not got  many many years to play with and it's  about time the government started to  live up to its own rhetoric  and take some of those crunchy decisions  filling the empty car parks with getaway  travellers will be  good for the economy but it doesn't fit  in with the prime minister's mantra  to build back green after the pandemic  lisa holland sky news east midlands  airport
 

